@startuml use-case-diagram-reviews-and-ratings
' ============================================================
' Use Case Package — "Reviews & Ratings"
'
' Owned by: Product Owner.
' Updated whenever a UC is added to / removed from / renamed in this package.
' Validated at M1: every UC-XXX file in this package must appear here exactly once,
'                  and every UC on this diagram must have a matching UC-XXX file.
'
' One overview per UC package. Use cases appearing on this diagram should fit
' the package's name. If a UC drifts outside the package theme, move it (or
' split the package).
' ============================================================

left to right direction
skinparam packageStyle rectangle
skinparam usecase {
  BackgroundColor #FEFEFE
  BorderColor #444444
}
skinparam shadowing false

' ── Actors ────────────────────────────────────────────────
actor "Customer"  as Cust
actor "Moderator" as Mod
actor "Editor"    as Ed

' ── Package boundary ──────────────────────────────────────
rectangle "Reviews & Ratings package" as Pkg {
  usecase "Write Customer Review\n(BS-UC-001)"        as UC001
  usecase "Moderate Customer Reviews\n(BS-UC-002)"    as UC002
  usecase "Show Book Reviews\n(BS-UC-003)"            as UC003
  usecase "Reject Customer Review\n(BS-UC-004)"       as UC004
  usecase "Write Editorial Review\n(BS-UC-005)"       as UC005
}

' Cross-package use case invoked from UC-001 alt-A
usecase "Login\n(BS-UC-100, Auth package)" as UC100

' ── Actor associations ────────────────────────────────────
Cust  --> UC001
Cust  --> UC003
Mod   --> UC002
Mod   --> UC004
Ed    --> UC005

' ── Use-case relationships ────────────────────────────────
UC001 ..> UC100 : <<include>>\n(when not logged in)
UC002 <.. UC004 : <<extend>>\n(when review is unacceptable)
UC003 ..> UC002 : <<requires>>\n(only published reviews shown)

note bottom of Pkg
  This thread's worked example details only BS-UC-001.
  BS-UC-002..005 are placeholders shown here so you can
  see how a package overview is meant to look once the
  package fills out.
end note

note right of UC100
  UC-100 lives in the Authentication
  package, not this one. It appears
  here only because BS-UC-001 invokes it.
end note

@enduml
